IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `fca-nouns`
IMPORT `fca-definitions`
IMPORT `fca-s3-application`
IMPORT `fca-s2-adjustment`

-- Frustrated Contracts Act 1959 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation -- THE SEAT LAYER (layer 2).
--
-- SEAT: a party to a contract that has become impossible to perform, asking, on facts they can state, what happens to money paid or
-- payable and to benefits already received, and whether the Act applies to the contract at all.  This layer phrases the rules already
-- encoded in the modules imported above as the questions that person would ask.  Nothing here is new law, and no rule is restated:
-- every answer is a call to a layer-1 function.  There is no seat for the court: where a provision addresses only the court (ss 2(3), (5),
-- (6), (7), 3(3), (4)) the person meets it as what the court may or must do with their money, their benefit or their contract.
--
-- PLAIN INPUTS.  Every question takes plain inputs -- yes/no, a number, a date, or a choice from a list -- one for each fact the person can
-- state, each with an @desc worded as the person would be asked.  The records the long modules expect are built inside each question
-- from those answers, and the SAME module rule is then called.
--   * What the court thinks (that it considers something just, that it has said how much it allows) is asked as "Yes / No / I am not sure";
--     "I am not sure" becomes NOTHING, and a rule that cannot answer without it answers NOTHING.  An amount the court has not yet fixed is
--     asked with a "has the court decided" yes/no beside it.
--   * A field that a question's rules never read gets a fixed placeholder, listed in the comment above that question ("not asked").
--   * The Act's own dates (1 January 1959, 13 February 1959) are inside the modules.  The Sale of Goods Act 1979 section 7 is another
--     Act that is not among the sources: whether it applies is a fact you state.
--
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT.  JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act itself gives; NOTHING means the Act does not answer on these facts
-- (see NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").  Never read NOTHING as no.  The order of the groups below is the Act's own
-- (s 2, then s 3); a form may well show the s 3 group ("Does the Act apply to my contract?") first.

DECLARE `My answer` IS ONE OF
    Yes
    No
    `I am not sure`

DECLARE `Kind of matter I ask about` IS ONE OF
    `The expenses the party who obtained the benefit incurred`
    `The effect of the circumstances that frustrated the contract on the benefit`
    `Some other circumstance of the case`
    `Something that is not a circumstance of the case`

DECLARE `The portion of my contract I ask about` IS ONE OF
    `The part treated as a separate contract`
    `The rest of my contract`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Wiring: the person's answers become the modules' inputs.  Not questions.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- An answer of Yes, No or "I am not sure" as a MAYBE BOOLEAN.
GIVEN answer IS A `My answer`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`what I say, as a fact` MEANS
    CONSIDER answer
    WHEN Yes THEN JUST TRUE -- input mapping
    WHEN No THEN JUST FALSE -- input mapping
    WHEN `I am not sure` THEN NOTHING -- input mapping

-- An amount the court may not yet have fixed: the yes/no says whether it has.
GIVEN decided IS A BOOLEAN
      amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`my amount if the court has decided` MEANS
    IF decided THEN JUST amount ELSE NOTHING -- input mapping

GIVEN matter IS A `Kind of matter I ask about`
GIVETH A `Matter for section 2(4)`
`the matter I ask about, in the Act's terms` MEANS
    CONSIDER matter
    WHEN `The expenses the party who obtained the benefit incurred` THEN `the expenses incurred before the time of discharge by the benefited party (paragraph (a))` -- input mapping
    WHEN `The effect of the circumstances that frustrated the contract on the benefit` THEN `the effect, in relation to that benefit, of the circumstances giving rise to the frustration of the contract (paragraph (b))` -- input mapping
    WHEN `Some other circumstance of the case` THEN `some other circumstance of the case` -- input mapping
    WHEN `Something that is not a circumstance of the case` THEN `something that is not a circumstance of the case` -- input mapping

GIVEN portion IS A `The portion of my contract I ask about`
GIVETH A `Portion of a contract`
`the portion in the Act's terms` MEANS
    CONSIDER portion
    WHEN `The part treated as a separate contract` THEN `the severed part` -- input mapping
    WHEN `The rest of my contract` THEN `the remainder of the contract` -- input mapping

-- The contract, from what the person knows.  The date the contract was made is read by no rule except s 3(1)'s one-answer rule, which
-- has its own question; here it is a fixed placeholder (1 January 2000).  The time of discharge is the date given if the parties
-- have been discharged, and is not given otherwise.
GIVEN frustrated IS A BOOLEAN
      discharged IS A BOOLEAN
      `date of discharge` IS A DATE
      government IS A BOOLEAN
      charterparty IS A BOOLEAN
      `time charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN
      `charterparty by way of demise` IS A BOOLEAN
      `carriage of goods by sea` IS A BOOLEAN
      insurance IS A BOOLEAN
      `section 7` IS A BOOLEAN
      `specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN
      perished IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Contract facts`
`my contract from what I know` MEANS
    `Contract facts` OF frustrated, discharged, (IF discharged THEN JUST `date of discharge` ELSE NOTHING), Date 1 1 2000, government, charterparty, `time charterparty`, `charterparty by way of demise`, `carriage of goods by sea`, insurance, `section 7`, `specific goods`, perished -- input mapping

-- The sums for the ceiling of s 2(3): the total paid and the total still to be paid, as the list of sums the layer-1 rule takes.
-- Each is one sum; whether they were paid, or payable, in pursuance of the contract before the discharge is what the person says of all of them.
GIVEN `total paid` IS A NUMBER
      `total still to pay` IS A NUMBER
      `every sum counted is within s 2(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A LIST OF `Sum facts`
`my sums taken together` MEANS
    LIST (`Sum facts` OF `total paid`, TRUE, `every sum counted is within s 2(2)`, `every sum counted is within s 2(2)`), (`Sum facts` OF `total still to pay`, FALSE, `every sum counted is within s 2(2)`, `every sum counted is within s 2(2)`) -- input mapping

§ `The Frustrated Contracts Act 1959 -- my questions`


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Does section 2 operate on my contract? (s 2(1))`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 2(1).  "Where" the contract has become impossible of performance or been otherwise frustrated and the parties have for that reason been discharged,
-- section 2 has effect, subject to section 3.  Yes if so; No if either "where" is not met or section 3 says the Act does not apply; NOTHING if it cannot be told.
-- Not asked (fixed placeholders; no rule this question reaches reads them): the Government is a party to my contract = FALSE; the date my contract was made = 1 January 2000.
@export Does section 2 of the Act have effect in relation to my contract (s 2(1))?
GIVEN `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your contract become impossible to perform, or been otherwise frustrated?
      `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the parties to your contract been discharged from performing it any further because of that?
      `the date the parties were discharged` IS A DATE @desc On what date were the parties discharged from performing the contract any further? (If they have not been discharged, give any date: it is not used.)
      `my contract is a charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a charterparty?
      `my charterparty is a time charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a time charterparty? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a charterparty by way of demise? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract for the carriage of goods by sea?
      `my contract is a contract of insurance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract of insurance?
      `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 apply to your contract? (That section avoids a contract for the sale of specific goods which perish before the risk has passed to the buyer.)
      `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods?
      `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract frustrated because the goods have perished?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does section 2 have effect on my contract` IS
    `s 2(1) section 2 has effect in relation to the contract` (`my contract from what I know` `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` `the date the parties were discharged` FALSE `my contract is a charterparty` `my charterparty is a time charterparty` `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` `my contract is a contract of insurance` `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished`)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Money paid, or payable, under the contract (s 2(2) and (3))`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 2(2).  Sums paid in pursuance of the contract before the parties were discharged are recoverable from the person who received them, subject to s 2(3).
-- Not asked (fixed placeholders; no rule this question reaches reads them): the Government is a party to my contract = FALSE; the date my contract was made = 1 January 2000.
@export Can I get back money I paid under the contract before it was discharged (s 2(2))?
GIVEN `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your contract become impossible to perform, or been otherwise frustrated?
      `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the parties to your contract been discharged from performing it any further because of that?
      `the date the parties were discharged` IS A DATE @desc On what date were the parties discharged from performing the contract any further? (If they have not been discharged, give any date: it is not used.)
      `my contract is a charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a charterparty?
      `my charterparty is a time charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a time charterparty? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a charterparty by way of demise? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract for the carriage of goods by sea?
      `my contract is a contract of insurance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract of insurance?
      `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 apply to your contract? (That section avoids a contract for the sale of specific goods which perish before the risk has passed to the buyer.)
      `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods?
      `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract frustrated because the goods have perished?
      `the amount of the sum` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the amount of the sum, in dollars?
      `the sum was paid or payable in pursuance of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the sum paid, or payable, in pursuance of the contract?
      `the sum was paid or fell payable before the parties were discharged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the sum paid, or did it fall payable, before the parties were discharged? (The Act says "before the time"; if it was on the very day of the discharge, answer according to which came first.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I get back money I paid` IS
    `s 2(2) a sum paid is recoverable from the party who received it` (`my contract from what I know` `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` `the date the parties were discharged` FALSE `my contract is a charterparty` `my charterparty is a time charterparty` `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` `my contract is a contract of insurance` `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished`) (`Sum facts` OF `the amount of the sum`, TRUE, `the sum was paid or payable in pursuance of the contract`, `the sum was paid or fell payable before the parties were discharged`)

-- s 2(2).  Sums payable in pursuance of the contract before the parties were discharged cease to be payable, subject to s 2(3).
-- Not asked (fixed placeholders; no rule this question reaches reads them): the Government is a party to my contract = FALSE; the date my contract was made = 1 January 2000.
@export Do sums I still had to pay under the contract before it was discharged stop being payable (s 2(2))?
GIVEN `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your contract become impossible to perform, or been otherwise frustrated?
      `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the parties to your contract been discharged from performing it any further because of that?
      `the date the parties were discharged` IS A DATE @desc On what date were the parties discharged from performing the contract any further? (If they have not been discharged, give any date: it is not used.)
      `my contract is a charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a charterparty?
      `my charterparty is a time charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a time charterparty? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a charterparty by way of demise? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract for the carriage of goods by sea?
      `my contract is a contract of insurance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract of insurance?
      `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 apply to your contract? (That section avoids a contract for the sale of specific goods which perish before the risk has passed to the buyer.)
      `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods?
      `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract frustrated because the goods have perished?
      `the amount of the sum` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the amount of the sum, in dollars?
      `the sum was paid or payable in pursuance of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the sum paid, or payable, in pursuance of the contract?
      `the sum was paid or fell payable before the parties were discharged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the sum paid, or did it fall payable, before the parties were discharged? (The Act says "before the time"; if it was on the very day of the discharge, answer according to which came first.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do sums I had to pay stop being payable` IS
    `s 2(2) a sum payable ceases to be payable` (`my contract from what I know` `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` `the date the parties were discharged` FALSE `my contract is a charterparty` `my charterparty is a time charterparty` `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` `my contract is a contract of insurance` `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished`) (`Sum facts` OF `the amount of the sum`, FALSE, `the sum was paid or payable in pursuance of the contract`, `the sum was paid or fell payable before the parties were discharged`)

-- s 2(3).  If the party who received (or was to receive) the sums incurred expenses before the discharge in, or for the purpose of, performing the contract,
-- the court may, if it considers it just, allow that party to keep (or recover) the whole or part of the sums.  Whether the court considers it just is the court's judgment: you tell us.
-- Not asked (fixed placeholders; no rule this question reaches reads them): the Government is a party to my contract = FALSE; the amount of the sum = 0; the amount of those expenses = 0; the sum the court includes for overhead expenses = 0; the sum the court includes for work or services performed personally = 0; the date my contract was made = 1 January 2000.
@export Can the court allow the other party to keep or recover part of the sums for the expenses they went to (s 2(3))?
GIVEN `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your contract become impossible to perform, or been otherwise frustrated?
      `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the parties to your contract been discharged from performing it any further because of that?
      `the date the parties were discharged` IS A DATE @desc On what date were the parties discharged from performing the contract any further? (If they have not been discharged, give any date: it is not used.)
      `my contract is a charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a charterparty?
      `my charterparty is a time charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a time charterparty? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a charterparty by way of demise? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract for the carriage of goods by sea?
      `my contract is a contract of insurance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract of insurance?
      `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 apply to your contract? (That section avoids a contract for the sale of specific goods which perish before the risk has passed to the buyer.)
      `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods?
      `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract frustrated because the goods have perished?
      `the sum was paid or payable in pursuance of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the sum paid, or payable, in pursuance of the contract?
      `the sum was paid or fell payable before the parties were discharged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the sum paid, or did it fall payable, before the parties were discharged? (The Act says "before the time"; if it was on the very day of the discharge, answer according to which came first.)
      `expenses were incurred before the parties were discharged in, or for the purpose of, performing the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the party who received the sum, or was to receive it, incur expenses before the parties were discharged in, or for the purpose of, performing the contract?
      `the court considers it just to allow the party to retain or recover the sum` IS A `My answer` @desc Does the court consider it just, having regard to all the circumstances of the case, to allow the party to retain or recover the sum? (Choose I am not sure if the court has not yet decided.)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the court allow the other party to keep or recover` IS
    `s 2(3) the court may allow the party to retain or recover the whole or part of the sums` (`my contract from what I know` `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` `the date the parties were discharged` FALSE `my contract is a charterparty` `my charterparty is a time charterparty` `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` `my contract is a contract of insurance` `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished`) (`Sum facts` OF 0, TRUE, `the sum was paid or payable in pursuance of the contract`, `the sum was paid or fell payable before the parties were discharged`) (`Expenses facts` OF `expenses were incurred before the parties were discharged in, or for the purpose of, performing the contract`, 0, 0, 0) (`what I say, as a fact` `the court considers it just to allow the party to retain or recover the sum`)

-- s 2(3).  Not more than the expenses incurred (as estimated under s 2(5)), and not more than the sums themselves.  NOTHING where the court has no power to allow anything (see the question before).
-- Not asked (fixed placeholders; no rule this question reaches reads them): the Government is a party to my contract = FALSE; the date my contract was made = 1 January 2000.
@export What is the most the court may allow the other party to keep or recover for their expenses, where there is only one sum (s 2(3))?
GIVEN `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your contract become impossible to perform, or been otherwise frustrated?
      `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the parties to your contract been discharged from performing it any further because of that?
      `the date the parties were discharged` IS A DATE @desc On what date were the parties discharged from performing the contract any further? (If they have not been discharged, give any date: it is not used.)
      `my contract is a charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a charterparty?
      `my charterparty is a time charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a time charterparty? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a charterparty by way of demise? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract for the carriage of goods by sea?
      `my contract is a contract of insurance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract of insurance?
      `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 apply to your contract? (That section avoids a contract for the sale of specific goods which perish before the risk has passed to the buyer.)
      `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods?
      `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract frustrated because the goods have perished?
      `the amount of the sum` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the amount of the sum, in dollars?
      `the sum was paid or payable in pursuance of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the sum paid, or payable, in pursuance of the contract?
      `the sum was paid or fell payable before the parties were discharged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the sum paid, or did it fall payable, before the parties were discharged? (The Act says "before the time"; if it was on the very day of the discharge, answer according to which came first.)
      `expenses were incurred before the parties were discharged in, or for the purpose of, performing the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the party who received the sum, or was to receive it, incur expenses before the parties were discharged in, or for the purpose of, performing the contract?
      `the amount of those expenses` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the amount of those expenses, in dollars? (0 if none)
      `the sum the court includes for overhead expenses` IS A NUMBER @desc What sum, in dollars, has the court included for overhead expenses? (0 if none)
      `the sum the court includes for work or services performed personally` IS A NUMBER @desc What sum, in dollars, has the court included for work or services performed personally by that party? (0 if none)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most the court may allow` IS
    `s 2(3) the most the court may allow to be retained or recovered` (`my contract from what I know` `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` `the date the parties were discharged` FALSE `my contract is a charterparty` `my charterparty is a time charterparty` `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` `my contract is a contract of insurance` `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished`) (`Sum facts` OF `the amount of the sum`, TRUE, `the sum was paid or payable in pursuance of the contract`, `the sum was paid or fell payable before the parties were discharged`) (`Expenses facts` OF `expenses were incurred before the parties were discharged in, or for the purpose of, performing the contract`, `the amount of those expenses`, `the sum the court includes for overhead expenses`, `the sum the court includes for work or services performed personally`)

-- s 2(3).  The amount allowed must not exceed the expenses incurred, and must be the whole or a part of the sums.
-- Not asked (fixed placeholders; no rule this question reaches reads them): the Government is a party to my contract = FALSE; the date my contract was made = 1 January 2000.
@export Is the amount the court allowed within the limit the Act sets, where there is only one sum (s 2(3))?
GIVEN `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your contract become impossible to perform, or been otherwise frustrated?
      `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the parties to your contract been discharged from performing it any further because of that?
      `the date the parties were discharged` IS A DATE @desc On what date were the parties discharged from performing the contract any further? (If they have not been discharged, give any date: it is not used.)
      `my contract is a charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a charterparty?
      `my charterparty is a time charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a time charterparty? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a charterparty by way of demise? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract for the carriage of goods by sea?
      `my contract is a contract of insurance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract of insurance?
      `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 apply to your contract? (That section avoids a contract for the sale of specific goods which perish before the risk has passed to the buyer.)
      `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods?
      `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract frustrated because the goods have perished?
      `the amount of the sum` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the amount of the sum, in dollars?
      `the sum was paid or payable in pursuance of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the sum paid, or payable, in pursuance of the contract?
      `the sum was paid or fell payable before the parties were discharged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the sum paid, or did it fall payable, before the parties were discharged? (The Act says "before the time"; if it was on the very day of the discharge, answer according to which came first.)
      `expenses were incurred before the parties were discharged in, or for the purpose of, performing the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the party who received the sum, or was to receive it, incur expenses before the parties were discharged in, or for the purpose of, performing the contract?
      `the amount of those expenses` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the amount of those expenses, in dollars? (0 if none)
      `the sum the court includes for overhead expenses` IS A NUMBER @desc What sum, in dollars, has the court included for overhead expenses? (0 if none)
      `the sum the court includes for work or services performed personally` IS A NUMBER @desc What sum, in dollars, has the court included for work or services performed personally by that party? (0 if none)
      `the amount the court allows` IS A NUMBER @desc How much, in dollars, has the court allowed? (0 if it has not decided or allowed nothing)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the amount the court allowed within the limit` IS
    `s 2(3) the amount the court allows is within the limit` (`my contract from what I know` `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` `the date the parties were discharged` FALSE `my contract is a charterparty` `my charterparty is a time charterparty` `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` `my contract is a contract of insurance` `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished`) (`Sum facts` OF `the amount of the sum`, TRUE, `the sum was paid or payable in pursuance of the contract`, `the sum was paid or fell payable before the parties were discharged`) (`Expenses facts` OF `expenses were incurred before the parties were discharged in, or for the purpose of, performing the contract`, `the amount of those expenses`, `the sum the court includes for overhead expenses`, `the sum the court includes for work or services performed personally`) `the amount the court allows`

-- s 2(3).  One ceiling for the sums paid and the sums payable together: not more than the expenses incurred (as estimated under s 2(5)), and not more than the sums themselves taken together.
-- Ask this one, not the one before it, when you paid some sums and still owed others.  The Act does not say in what order the sums count, and the answer is the same in any order.  NOTHING where the court has no power to allow anything.
-- Not asked (fixed placeholders; no rule this question reaches reads them): the Government is a party to my contract = FALSE; the date my contract was made = 1 January 2000.
@export What is the most the court may allow the other party to keep or recover for their expenses, taking together the sums I paid and the sums I still had to pay (s 2(3))?
GIVEN `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your contract become impossible to perform, or been otherwise frustrated?
      `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the parties to your contract been discharged from performing it any further because of that?
      `the date the parties were discharged` IS A DATE @desc On what date were the parties discharged from performing the contract any further? (If they have not been discharged, give any date: it is not used.)
      `my contract is a charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a charterparty?
      `my charterparty is a time charterparty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a time charterparty? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your contract is a charterparty, is it a charterparty by way of demise? (Answer No if it is not a charterparty.)
      `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract for the carriage of goods by sea?
      `my contract is a contract of insurance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract a contract of insurance?
      `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 apply to your contract? (That section avoids a contract for the sale of specific goods which perish before the risk has passed to the buyer.)
      `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods?
      `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your contract frustrated because the goods have perished?
      `the total of the sums I paid` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the total, in dollars, of the sums you paid under the contract before the parties were discharged? (0 if none)
      `the total of the sums I still had to pay` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the total, in dollars, of the sums that had fallen payable by you before the parties were discharged but that you had not paid? (0 if none)
      `every sum I have counted was paid or payable in pursuance of the contract before the parties were discharged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was every sum you have included in those two totals paid, or payable, in pursuance of the contract before the parties were discharged? (Leave out of the totals any sum that was not.)
      `expenses were incurred before the parties were discharged in, or for the purpose of, performing the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the party who received the sum, or was to receive it, incur expenses before the parties were discharged in, or for the purpose of, performing the contract?
      `the amount of those expenses` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the amount of those expenses, in dollars? (0 if none)
      `the sum the court includes for overhead expenses` IS A NUMBER @desc What sum, in dollars, has the court included for overhead expenses? (0 if none)
      `the sum the court includes for work or services performed personally` IS A NUMBER @desc What sum, in dollars, has the court included for work or services performed personally by that party? (0 if none)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most the court may allow from all the sums together` IS
    `s 2(3) the most the court may allow to be retained or recovered from the sums taken together` (`my contract from what I know` `my contract has become impossible to perform or been otherwise frustrated` `the parties have been discharged from performing my contract any further` `the date the parties were discharged` FALSE `my contract is a charterparty` `my charterparty is a time charterparty` `my charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise` `my contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` `my contract is a contract of insurance` `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to my contract` `my contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` `my contract is frustrated because the goods have perished`) (`my sums taken together` `the total of the sums I paid` `the total of the sums I still had to pay` `every sum I have counted was paid or payable in pursuance of the contract before the parties were discharged`) (`Expenses facts` OF `expenses were incurred before the parties were discharged in, or for the purpose of, performing the contract`, `the amount of those expenses`, `the sum the court includes for overhead expenses`, `the sum the court includes for work or services performed personally`)

-- s 2(3).  The amount allowed out of all the sums together must not exceed the expenses incurred, and must be the whole or a part of the sums taken together.
-- Not asked (fixed placeholders; no rule this question reaches reads them): the Government is a party to my contract = FALSE; the date my contract was made = 1 January 2000.
@export Is the amount the court allowed, out of the sums I paid and the sums I still had to pay taken together, within the limit the Act sets (s 2(3))?
